description:
Split out of ub-og3 by Miguel's decision at its Decide phase (2026-08-06), fork "resilience": ub-og3 is a pure CHANNEL fix (where the failure report is written); this issue is the ROOT CAUSE of one of ub-og3's failure cases (why there is a failure at all). It has its own decision and its own PR.

CORRECTED 2026-08-06 at the ub-og3 design Review gate. This issue was first written claiming that ANY first frame other than `initialize` kills the server. That is FALSE: `ping` is answered pre-handshake. The corrected class is below, established both by reading rmcp and by measurement.

WHAT RMCP ACTUALLY DOES, read at rmcp-1.7.0/src/service/server.rs:170-204 (the comment there cites the MCP lifecycle specification, which permits `ping` before `initialize`):
- a pre-`initialize` `ping` REQUEST is answered with an empty result and the loop CONTINUES waiting for `initialize` (:175-189);
- any other REQUEST breaks the loop (:191) and, unless it is `initialize`, returns `ExpectedInitializeRequest` (:200-204) — the server dies;
- any NON-Request message (a notification) returns the same error at :193 — the server dies.

MEASURED off main at 7790db0, one spawn per case, confirming all three arms:
- `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"ping"}` first → answered `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{}}`, and a following `initialize` completes a normal handshake, exit 0;
- `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"notifications/cancelled"}` first → server dies, exit 1;
- `{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":7,"method":"tools/list"}` first → server dies, exit 1.

So the class is: any first frame that is neither `initialize` nor `ping`, whether Request or Notification. A sloppy or hostile client therefore kills a server that has already opened a healthy workspace and taken the D31 write lock.

WHY IT IS FIXABLE. rmcp's strictness beyond the specification's `ping` allowance is its own choice. The repo already owns the read framing: the D47 answer-and-drop arm in crates/unblock-mcp/src/wire.rs:333-364 intercepts a frame whose envelope id cannot be decoded, answers it out-of-band and DROPS it precisely so it never reaches that rmcp slot. The same seam is the candidate mechanism here.

THREE COSTS, sized by the ub-og3 Understand phase and to be confirmed by this issue's own Understand:
1. the gate needs handshake state (has `initialize` completed?) that the transport does not currently carry;
2. it breaks the CD-7 differential harness as written — crates/unblock-mcp/src/wire.rs:756-804 drives a BARE transport, so a pre-handshake gate would apply to the whole parity corpus;
3. it destroys the provocation of the shipped D38 cell at crates/unblock-cli/tests/mcp_lifecycle.rs:407-441, which uses exactly this frame to produce its genuine `Err`, with the coupling documented at :578-583. No obvious replacement provocation exists.

SCOPE NOTE. This helps only the non-`initialize`-frame case. The other ub-og3 cases (workspace open fails, corrupt db, schema newer, bad output-format env) never reach the transport at all.

DECIDE FIRST, before implementing: what a server SHOULD do with a pre-`initialize` request and with a pre-`initialize` notification per the MCP specification, and whether the answer differs between the two — noting that the specification already singles `ping` out, so the question is how far past `ping` to go. Footprint is expected to be comparable to D47: its own D-id, its own PR.
